<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Genesee River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Genesee River rises in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and flows north through western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> to Lake <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a> at Rochester. Its valley was the heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>, westernmost nation of the Iroquois Confederacy, whose towns there were burned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sullivan-expedition">Sullivan Expedition</a> in 1779. After the Revolution the fertile valley was sold to settlers, and the river's falls powered the mills of Rochester.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genesee-river.jpg" alt="Genesee River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pollinator at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genesee_river_9044.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Genesee flows north through the Twin Tiers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>. Its rock layers tilt so that the river crosses ever older bedrock as it goes, from the sandstones and shales of the Allegheny Plateau to Devonian shales and limestones in the gorges of Letchworth State Park, where it falls three times over 600 feet in all, and Silurian rocks where it cuts through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niagara-escarpment">Niagara Escarpment</a> at Rochester. The many exposed layers make the valley rich in fossils.</p><p>Glaciers reshaped the river. About 12,000 years ago moraines diverted its ancient eastern branch toward the Susquehanna, and its original outlet, Irondequoit Bay, was flooded; as the waters fell, glacial debris turned the lower river west onto its present course.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The river's gorge long marked the border between the Haudenosaunee to the east and the Erie and Wenrohronon peoples to the west. Thomas Davies surveyed the region in 1766, when early writers also spelled the river Zinochsaa. In 1779, on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>'s orders, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sullivan-expedition">Sullivan Expedition</a> destroyed over 40 Haudenosaunee villages in and around the watershed. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-big-tree">Treaty of Big Tree</a> of 1797 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a> kept six reservations along the river, but in August 1826 the Ogden Land Company bought them, allegedly under duress; the Seneca Nation of Indians does not recognise the sale.</p><p>From 1801 to 1846 the lands west of the river, part of the Holland Purchase, were sold to settlers from the Holland Land Office in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>. Water power drew flour mills, clothing mills and toolmakers to Rochester, and from 1825 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a>, which crossed the river on an aqueduct of 1823, carried their goods to New York City. The Genesee Valley Canal, begun in 1836, was plagued by floods and abandoned in 1880; its route became a railroad and later the Genesee Valley Greenway.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The March 1865 thaw brought the worst flood in Rochester's history, and the flood of Hurricane Agnes in 1972 broke all county records. The Mount Morris Dam, built in the 1950s and the largest flood control dam east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, now controls the floods, and the river still provides hydroelectric power for downtown Rochester.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Genesee River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Haudenosaunee, the People of the Longhouse, were Iroquoian-speaking farming peoples of what is now upstate New York, living in palisaded villages of bark longhouses between the Hudson River and the <b>Genesee</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Seneca lived in western New York between Seneca Lake and the <b>Genesee River</b>, the westernmost of the five founding Haudenosaunee nations.</blockquote><a href="/state/seneca-nation">Seneca Nation</a></li></ul></section>
